These sections compare glowcap with libraries you may already know. Each one
times one application in glowcap and in another system on the same
machine, in the same sitting, with test programs of the same shape. The
[performance page]($link.page('performance')) holds glowcap's own costs and
the budgets the build pins.

```=html
<!-- bench:stamp GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<p class="bench-stamp">Benchmark snapshot of source <code>56b1a791f</code> on 2026-10-26: Linux aarch64, 20 logical CPUs, 122 GiB RAM, Racket v9.3 CS, Spritely Goblins v0.18.0-52-gdcc2b2c5, and the <code>glowcap</code> archives built by Zig 0.17.0 in ReleaseFast. <code>zig build bench-site-report</code> repeats it.</p>
```

# [A call beside Cap'n Proto RPC]($section.id('a-call-beside-capn-proto-rpc'))

[Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) RPC grew from the same ideas as CapTP, the protocol glowcap implements:
calls pass object references, and a call can go to an answer that has not
arrived. `bench-capnp` times two calls in the C++ reference library and
in the `capnp-rpc` Rust crate. `greet` is the smallest message that is still
a call. `report` is one application batch of a name, eight tags, and
sixty-four readings, which the server sums. glowcap's bars are the same two
calls over its `tcp` transport, once under its [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup)
codec and once under its Cap'n Proto codec.

Every bench runs a client thread and a server thread in one process over
loopback TCP. The client makes one call at a time, and both threads sleep
between calls, so every bar pays the same wakeups. The first group pins both
threads to one core and the second gives each its own core.

Every client builds the request, sends it, and reads the reply's fields
inside the timed window, and compares them after the clock stops. The C++
bench and its generated code are compiled with full optimization (`-O3`), and
the Cap'n Proto library it links keeps its own build settings. The Rust row
runs the `capnp-rpc` crate on a current-thread tokio runtime through the
futures compat layer.

Each figure is the median (p50) of 3,000 calls from the run the stamp at the top
names. The
[benchmarks README](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/bench/README.md#against-capn-proto)
holds the repeated-run figures, their spread, and the system calls and
instructions behind one round trip.

