<thead><tr><th>actors</th><th>glowcap snapshot µs</th><th>Goblins snapshot µs</th><th>glowcap restore µs</th><th>Goblins restore µs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>64</td><td>1</td><td>6.3</td><td>2.6</td><td>3.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>256</td><td>5.5</td><td>34</td><td>12.5</td><td>12.6</td></tr>
<tr><td>1024</td><td>39.9</td><td>124.5</td><td>127</td><td>61.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>4096</td><td>126.2</td><td>565.8</td><td>365.1</td><td>247.7</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p class="bench-note">Both runtimes snapshot in O(objects) and stay flat as each actor grows larger (16.9 µs for 64-byte actors, 30.1 µs for 16384-byte actors). They share each actor's state instead of copying it. The runtimes differ, so their absolute times are not directly comparable. The shared O(objects) trend is the result. On restore, glowcap frees the rewound copies right away, and Goblins leaves that cleanup to its garbage collector.</p>
